+You are the data-visualization and front-end engineer `fix-viz-5` for melon-seek (repo /home/user/melon-seek, branch claude/stoic-ride-54ddxp), a zero-build vanilla ES-module app, "the Zillow of job postings". You are in your own git worktree. First run `git checkout --detach fbdad58` (the lead's integration head for fix wave 5: phone, pay-at-place, data-3 and sec-3 lanes merged; it is in the shared object store, and the lead will tell you when a newer pushed head exists). Read docs/CONTRACT.md (the fix-pay-at-place and fix-data-3 sections), docs/strategy/ROADMAP.md §8 (UI simplicity budget), docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md (including "Checkpoints" and "Known traps"), docs/process/TEMPLATE.md, docs/process/viz-review-beta-2.md, docs/process/ux-review-beta-2.md, docs/process/viz-even.md and docs/process/fix-pay-at-place.md.
+
+**The user, verbatim:**
+- 2026-10-11: "Are you sure the salary range of the big tech are correct?! They don't look correct and kinda all span too evenly." viz-even fixed the bin-centred circles and stacked identical bars; V-33, V-34 and V-38 below are the rest of it.
+- 2026-10-16: "Whatever was done with the ui/UX it needs to go through the mobile and desktop QC QA again. The mobile page on the beta site is totally broken".
+- 2026-10-16: "Make sure we check for all phone sizes from iPhone mini to razr to duo or something".
+- Standing: present pay as posted, never cut or hide a posting or a posted pay row ("we must present them as they are and not cut anything"); big-tech figures are base pay only, and say so.
+
+**Your findings** (evidence, repro links and screenshots in the logs named; red tests in test/e2e/beta-viz-review-2.e2e.js, test/e2e/beta-a11y-2.e2e.js and test/e2e/beta-desktop-qa-3.e2e.js where they exist):
+1. **The rest of "spans too evenly":**
+   - V-33 (viz-review-beta-2): Clusters' circle plan never settles on Amazon (the `for (let pass …)` loop in `renderClusters` alternates) and merges two real pay peaks ($87.4K × 1,123 and $107.7K × 542 in "Operations, IT, & Support Engineering"). Plan at the drawn sizes, and stop on a settled plan.
+   - V-34: 42–92% of circles are the 9 px floor, so 1 and 123 postings look the same (`radiusAt`, `rMin`). A circle's size or a visible mark must tell a crowded circle from a one-posting one.
+   - V-38 and UX-41 (ux-review-beta-2): Ranges rows of 775 postings and of one look alike, and a stacked row says its count twice ("14 roles" and "×14") with no title. Make the busy rows read as busy (e.g. bar thickness or a count bar that scales with `p.n`, within the row height budget), and give a stacked row its title.
+   - Measure before and after with docs/process/scripts/viz-even-measure.mjs and the reviewer's numbers (floor-size share per board, rows-vs-postings share by $25K band), and put both tables in your log.
+2. **Pay as posted, in the drawing:**
+   - V-41: the chart doesn't mark posted minimums ("from €46K", `salary.kind: 'minimum'`) or pay that includes the top shift differential (`includesShiftDifferential`); `rangeKey()` stacks a minimum with an exact salary of the same amount. The tooltips and bars must say what the cards say.
+   - MR-28 remainder (fix-data-3, docs/process/methodology-review-beta-2.md §MR-28): the client medians (the chart's row medians, the stats line, Insights) still pool stated minimums, shift-inclusive hourly pay and part-time hourly pay with base pay. scripts/build-market.js `notBasePay` leaves them out of the server cells; use the same rule in the browser (one shared helper in public/features/shared.js, used by every median), keep every posting on the chart, and say in the notes' Details what the medians leave out.
+   - V-48: on-target-earnings bars (dashed) have no legend key, and OTE level spans are drawn striped (a span wins over OTE in `buildRow()`).
+   - D-43 (qa-desktop-beta-3): the chart rounds range ends in the millions to one decimal ("$600K–$1.1M") where the card says "$600K–1.07M". Use the card's formatter.
+3. **Axes, legends, labels:**
+   - V-46: on a phone nothing on the chart says the axis is US dollars a year; the narrow caption and the median caption in `renderAxis()`/`renderHist()` are dead code there (`labelW > 0`). Show them on phones, on one line, at every size.
+   - UX-59: Ranges' first screen on Amazon runs every bar off the right edge (the axis ends at P99 of midpoints while the list leads with the highest pay). Take the domain from the range ends the rows show.
+   - UX-40: chart row sub-lines mix "1,215 · $178K US" and "759 roles · median $161K in the US". One form.
+   - UX-42: counts wider than their circles (Microsoft "118" 22.2 px in a 21.5 px circle). UX-43: a bare "›"/"‹" edge chip for one posting reads as "next"; say the count ("›1").
+   - UX-36: Ranges reads "Group: None" (phone: "None"). Plain words.
+   - UX-61 (the chart's part only): one currency name in the axis caption ("US$ a year").
+   - V-42: the Clusters P25–P75 band is 1.1:1 against the chart in light (1.17–1.35:1 dark), and its values are written nowhere. Give it a visible contrast and put the quartiles in the row's accessible name/title.
+   - A-8 (security-a11y-review-beta-2, red test "[A-8] beta a11y 2"): the "N more departments" fold row puts aria-expanded on role=option (axe `aria-allowed-attr`, critical).
+   - D-44: Group by Location opens with one- and two-role places ranked by their single posting's pay (8 of Google's first 10 rows hold 1–2 roles; Mountain View, 500, is row 11). Order or fold so the big places lead, and keep every place reachable.
+   - Cuts found by fix-phone-text's density guard and the phone matrix (allow-listed there as "viz lane"; remove them from the allow-list when fixed): the Ranges legend keys (viz.css `.ms-legend__item` max-width 220px) cut "Operations, IT, & Support Engineering"; at 280 px the chart row fold arrow `.ms-crow__fold` is cut; at 280 px the map legend "Median pay" wraps to 3 lines; the Ranges axis caption is cut at 540 px (Surface Duo, one screen); Amazon department names in chart rows that start alike must stay distinguishable on phones (fix-data-3 renamed them, "Technical …" / "Non-Technical …"; check, UX-38's phone half).
+4. **Map:**
+   - V-43: a pin priced from 1% of its postings reads as their median ("$76K · 434" from 5 with pay). Say on the pin when the price rests on few of its postings (a share as well as a count, `MIN_PIN_PAY`).
+   - V-44: 9 Meta postings with no place are on no pin, not in Remote, and not counted. Count them in the map's notes (never drop them).
+   - V-45 remainder: `isApprox()` flags only 'centroid', not 'country' precision. fix-data-3 placed Cajamar and Luxembourg City; check what is left and fix the mark.
+   - From fix-phone-sizes: on the Razr cover screen (352x355) the third pin is under the map's own controls.
+5. **V-47 (speed, no test: it depends on load).** Amazon desktop: a filter, Group by or a view switch takes 0.3–0.6 s, of which the chart's `update()` is 37–137 ms. Cut the chart's share where you can (no change to what is drawn); measure before and after, 3 runs each, at a stated load average.
